<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Resistance Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1981">1981</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Resistance Army was the armed wing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>'s National Resistance Movement, which waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war, the Bush War, against the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tito-okello">Tito Okello</a> from 1981. Growing from a small band of fighters, it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> in January 1986 and brought Museveni to power. It then became Uganda's national army and was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-defence-force">Uganda People's Defence Force</a> in 1995.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The army was formed in 1981 by the merger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>'s Popular Resistance Army with the Uganda Freedom Fighters, the group of the former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-lule">Yusuf Lule</a>. Museveni, then leader of the Uganda Patriotic Movement party, alleged electoral fraud after the Uganda Peoples Congress won the bitterly disputed general election of 1980, and declared an armed rebellion. He had gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> experience with the Mozambican Liberation Front, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, and had formed his own Front for National Salvation, FRONASA, in Tanzania to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>. The army was supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugandan-bush-war">Ugandan Bush War</a> the army fought the government troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-national-liberation-army">Uganda National Liberation Army</a>, the UNLA, and defeated them in 1986. When it entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> it had about 16,000 combatants, about a quarter of them Banyarwanda, and its deputy commander was Fred Rwigema. It then became the national army of Uganda.</p><p>From 1986 to 1990 Museveni's government tried to end a number of insurgencies and to establish its control over the army. It repeatedly claimed that the National Resistance Army had defeated the UNLA and other rebel groups, but insurgent activity went on, above all in the northern, eastern and western regions. In April 1988, 3,000 former fighters of the Uganda People's Army and of several smaller rebel groups accepted a government amnesty, surrendered and declared their support for Museveni.</p><p>In June 1988 Museveni concluded a peace agreement with Lieutenant Colonel John Angelo Okello, commander of the Uganda People's Democratic Army. The National Resistance Army took many fighters of both groups into its ranks, but thousands of others rejected the accord and went on fighting against it. After the Ugandan constitution of 1995 was enacted, the army was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-defence-force">Uganda People's Defence Force</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Resistance Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rwandan exiles who fought in Museveni’s <b>National Resistance Army</b>, among them Paul Kagame, went on to lead the Rwandan Patriotic Front, which invaded Rwanda from Uganda in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
